// The world's resources, 2024: what the harvest, the mines and the fabs are actually made of.
//
// Thirty-nine goods could say that a country had "food" and "electronics". They could not say
// that Egypt eats Ukrainian wheat, that Europe's sunflower oil came through Odesa, that nine
// chips in ten of the most advanced kind are made on one island, that every one of those fabs
// runs on a Dutch machine, that a steelworks without South African manganese makes nothing,
// that China mines four-fifths of the tungsten and nearly all of the gallium, or that a
// Belarusian potash embargo is felt in an Indian harvest. These twenty-four can, and each of
// them is in the economy rather than beside it: made somewhere (common/production and the
// deposits in history/states/2024/zz_world_resources.json), wanted by something, traded, and
// - through a recipe's `needs` or `shortfall` - missed when it does not come.
//
// Food stays the good households eat. The raw harvest - grain, oilseeds, sugar, fish - is what
// Food is made of, traded on its own, so the countries that grow it and the countries that
// import it are different countries, as they are.
//
// Grain is priced on its trade (`"world_price": "traded"`): importers' wants against exporters'
// spare, not the whole harvest, most of which never leaves the country that grows it. Its base
// price is set so that the 2024 price, at the trade's opening tightness, is where it was.
//
// `leverage` is what a seller can do with it (CommodityLeverage): "strategic" goods are levers,
// "high_tech" ones count twice. Abstract weekly market units and prices in billions, like every
// other good. Essential weights are kept small: a shortage of tungsten is felt through the
// shells it did not make, not by the household directly; grain and fuel are the exceptions.
